## Platform policy

Shopify is the default and the priority. WooCommerce is supported but will be a small
fraction of brands, so it gets a small fraction of the library: 1 good source, not 4.
Woo brands get the knowledge layer and a brief a human executes, not automated theme
deploys or test purchases. `platform` is an exception tag everywhere else, but almost
nothing in SET transfers between platforms, so tag every source here.

## Topics

domain and email, store configuration, payments and tax settings, shipping profiles,
tracking install, legal pages, verification steps.

## Look for

Exact click paths and settings. What breaks if done in the wrong order. Verification
steps that prove a step actually worked. Platform documentation is tier 1 and wins; a
walkthrough is useful for the order of operations only.

## Skip

Anything over 18 months old, because interfaces change and a stale click path is
worse than none. AI-builds-your-store demos, which decay in months and teach a product
rather than a mechanism. Multiple beginner tutorials covering the same setup: keep the
official one plus at most 1.

## Scope mix

Nearly all baseline.

